     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order, even with your confirmation. 3000 mg three times daily is 9,000 mg/day. For a 70 kg patient, that is close to the range where acetaminophen causes acute liver injury (about 150 mg/kg, or roughly 10.5 g). Giving it on a schedule would put the patient at serious risk of harm. I don't know of any indication that justifies it.\n\nHere are some ways forward:\n- **Enter a standard dose.** I can enter 1000 mg orally three or four times daily (3,000 to 4,000 mg/day), or another regimen you choose.\n- **Consult pharmacy or toxicology.** If you're trying to reach a particular clinical goal, they can suggest options, such as multimodal analgesia or an adjunct for fever or pain.\n- **Check for a mix-up.** The 3000 mg may be a daily total that got entered as a per-dose amount.\n\nIf you still believe this dose is warranted, it would need to go through your institution's pharmacist-verified override process. I can't place it for you. Which standard regimen would you like me to enter?"
      }
     ],
